A 54-year-old male with long-standing severe alcohol use disorder and decompensated cirrhosis with tense ascites and jaundice presents to the emergency department in a lethargic, confused state with asterixis, diagnostic of hepatic encephalopathy. Five days prior, his outpatient diuretic regimen was aggressively escalated by adding high-dose furosemide (80 mg daily) to his baseline spironolactone (100 mg daily) and hydrochlorothiazide (25 mg daily) to treat refractory ascites. Physical examination reveals deep jaundice, spider angiomas, dry mucous membranes, marked abdominal distension with a fluid wave, and profound asterixis. Urgent laboratory evaluation demonstrates: serum sodium = 132 mEq/L, serum potassium = 2.5 mEq/L (severe hypokalemia), serum chloride = 84 mEq/L, arterial blood gas pH = 7.56, pCO2 = 46 mm Hg, serum bicarbonate = 38 mEq/L (severe hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis), BUN = 48 mg/dL, serum creatinine = 1.8 mg/dL (baseline 0.8 mg/dL), and venous blood ammonia = 168 micromol/L (normal: 15-45 micromol/L).

Trace the complete 5-step pathophysiology and pharmacological sequence that links aggressive multi-class diuretic therapy, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, and the precipitous neurotoxic exacerbation of hepatic encephalopathy:
1. The combined transport mechanisms of high-dose loop and thiazide diuretics that accelerate distal fluid/solute delivery and amplify secondary hyperaldosteronism, driving profound collecting duct kaliuresis and proton extrusion;
2. The transcellular ion-shift buffering response across systemic and renal tubular epithelial cell membranes during severe hypokalemia (intracellular potassium exiting in exchange for extracellular hydrogen ions);
3. The enzymatic and metabolic stimulation inside proximal tubular epithelial cells triggered by intracellular acidosis (activation of mitochondrial glutaminase and glutamate dehydrogenase), driving accelerated renal ammoniagenesis from glutamine;
4. The biophysical and physicochemical diffusion of lipid-soluble non-ionized ammonia (NH3) versus water-soluble charged ammonium (NH4+) across the alkaline blood-brain barrier under systemic alkalemic conditions (pH 7.56 vs. pKa ~9.2);
5. The integrated emergency therapeutic sequence required to halt neurotoxicity and resolve encephalopathy (immediate cessation of all loop and thiazide diuretics, aggressive parenteral potassium chloride repletion to extinguish renal ammoniagenesis, and oral lactulose administration to trap ammonium in the colonic lumen).

