A 59-year-old male with long-standing alcoholic cirrhosis complicated by tense ascites, portal hypertension, and splenomegaly is brought to the emergency department in a state of profound delirium and visual hallucinations. Five days ago, in an attempt to treat rapidly worsening ascites at home, the patient doubled his furosemide dose (from 40 mg to 80 mg twice daily) and added over-the-counter high-dose chlorpheniramine and diphenhydramine for insomnia. Vital signs on presentation: blood pressure 92/58 mm Hg, heart rate 118/min (orthostatic), respiratory rate 14/min, and temperature 36.8 °C. Physical examination reveals severe temporal muscle wasting, dry mucous membranes, marked abdominal distension with shifting dullness, and a coarse, flapping tremor of the dorsiflexed wrists (asterixis). An urgent ECG reveals prolonged PR intervals, flattened T waves, and prominent U waves in leads V2-V4. Urgent laboratory evaluation demonstrates a severe multi-electrolyte and acid-base derangement: serum sodium = 129 mEq/L, serum potassium = 2.3 mEq/L (severe hypokalemia), serum chloride = 81 mEq/L, arterial blood gas pH = 7.58, pCO2 = 47 mm Hg, serum bicarbonate = 42 mEq/L (severe hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis), BUN = 54 mg/dL, serum creatinine = 2.1 mg/dL (baseline 0.8 mg/dL; acute kidney injury superimposed on cirrhosis), and venous blood ammonia = 184 micromol/L (normal: 15-45 micromol/L).

Trace the complete 5-step pathophysiology, molecular transport, biochemical, and neurotoxic sequence that links aggressive loop diuresis to this life-threatening hepatic coma:
1. The thick ascending limb transport mechanism of high-dose furosemide (NKCC2 blockade) and the subsequent downstream hormonal and electrical forces (cirrhotic secondary hyperaldosteronism, accelerated flow) that drive massive collecting duct kaliuresis and H+ secretion;
2. The transcellular ion-exchange buffering response triggered across all systemic and renal tubular epithelial cell membranes by severe extracellular hypokalemia (cellular K+ efflux in exchange for extracellular H+ influx);
3. The resulting paradoxical intracellular acidosis inside proximal convoluted tubule epithelial cells and its direct enzymatic stimulation of mitochondrial glutaminase and glutamate dehydrogenase, accelerating renal ammoniagenesis from glutamine;
4. The biophysical shift in the Henderson-Hasselbalch equilibrium in the setting of severe systemic alkalemia (pH 7.58 vs. pKa ~9.2) that converts charged, impermeable ammonium (NH4+) into uncharged, lipid-soluble non-ionized ammonia (NH3), which rapidly crosses the blood-brain barrier to trigger astrocyte osmotic swelling and encephalopathy; and
5. The integrated evidence-based emergency critical care resuscitation sequence required to reverse this neuro-metabolic crisis (immediate discontinuation of loop diuretics, aggressive parenteral potassium chloride repletion to resolve proximal tubular intracellular acidosis and extinguish renal ammoniagenesis, cautious volume expansion, and lactulose therapy to trap ammonia in the stool).

